      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To study effects of pacemaker implantation on the course of coronary heart disease (CHD) with stable angina pectoris and choice of optimal regimen of pacing."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 154 CHD patients with a pacemaker were examined. All the patients had angina of effort of functional class II-IV."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The symptoms of the disease improved in 72 (46.8%) patients (group 1): the number of anginal attacks decreased, exercise tolerance increased, the dose of antianginal medicines went down. Pain attacks became more frequent, response to nitroglycerin changed in 30 (19.5%) patients of group 2. This was explained by 1.5-2-fold enhancement of heart rate by pacemaker raising myocardial oxygen consumption and psychocardial syndrome. In 52 (33.8%) patients of group 3 anginal attacks characteristics did not change."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "To optimize coronary reserve, frequency of electroimpulses must be reprogrammed to adjust to a functional class of angina and chronic cardiac failure as well as pacing regime. In particular, low coronary reserve demands optimal frequency of 55-65 imp/min while congenital cardiac failure--75-85 imp/min."
        }
      ]},
